  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Wednesday)\nIssued at 229 PM CDT THU JUL 23 2026\n\nGuidance and NHC continue to keep Tropical Storm Bertha moving\nwestward away from Alabama toward the Southeast Texas Coast\ntonight. Meanwhile, a quasi-stationary front continues to meander\nacross the Southeast and Tennessee Valley, tapping into deep\ntropical moisture to support widespread, heavy showers and\nthunderstorms. This pattern sustains an ongoing risk for high\nrain rates and flash flooding across the region through Friday\nand into Saturday, with training convective clusters remaining a\nprimary concern where precipitable water values exceed 2 inches.\n\nFor Central Alabama, this stalled boundary maintains an\nunsettled, wet regime through early Saturday, with a Flood Watch\npersisting for far northeast counties. While widespread cloud\ncover and rain will suppress temperatures through the weekend, a\nsignificant pattern shift arrives next week. As an upper-level\nridge expands across the South-Central U.S. and into the\nSoutheast, temperatures will trend significantly hotter,\nintroducing the potential for Moderate to Major HeatRisk by\nMonday and likely persisting into mid-week.\n\n16",
